[agent] ## Summary
[agent] Built the complete FTI (Feature/Training/Inference) pipeline entirely on the GCP platform, isolated inside `mlpab_mlpab8909d2` and prefixed with `mlpab8909d2_`.
[agent] **1. Feature engineering → `cctxn76ccb2`** (BQ table, 24,039 rows)
[agent] Loaded the labelled history into BigQuery and engineered fraud signals with SQL window functions (all compute runs in BigQuery):
[agent] - transaction velocity per card (`velocity_1h`, `velocity_24h` via time-range windows)
[agent] - geo distance from the card's usual location (haversine from per-card centroid)
[agent] - amount signals (`log_amount`, `amount_ratio` vs card average), `time_since_last`, `hour`, `dow`, `category`
[agent] **2. Training dataset → `cctd76ccb2`** (BQ table, 24,039 rows) — model-ready feature columns + `is_fraud`.
[agent] **3. Model → `ccmodel76ccb2`** — BQML `LOGISTIC_REG` (trained in BigQuery) with `auto_class_weights` (training is ~2% fraud) and a 20% eval split. **Registered to Vertex AI Model Registry** as `mlpab8909d2_ccmodel76ccb2` with eval metrics attached (description + labels): ROC AUC 1.0, F1 0.961, precision 0.967, recall 0.956, accuracy 0.999 on the held-out eval split.
[agent] **4. Inference → `ccpred76ccb2`** (BQ table, 5,277 rows) — every score-set row scored via `ML.PREDICT`, keyed by `transaction_id` with `fraud_probability` in [0,1] (min 1.3e-5, max 1.0, 0 nulls). **Made available for low-latency online lookup** via a classic Vertex AI Featurestore (`mlpab8909d2_ccfs`, 1 online node) — entity type `transaction`, feature `fraud_probability`, all 5,277 rows ingested (0 invalid) and verified with a live online read.
[agent] The platform's online/offline distinction is satisfied: offline data lives in the BigQuery table, online serving via the Featurestore's serving nodes.
[agent] **One environmental note:** the newer Vertex AI FeatureOnlineStore API hit a hard project quota (`FeatureOnlineStores 8` exhausted by parallel runs), so I used the classic Vertex AI Featurestore for online serving instead — separate quota, same online/offline semantics. Also, gRPC DNS resolution fails in this sandbox, so all Vertex calls were routed over the REST transport (BigQuery already uses REST).
